# 7. Why this is genuinely Algorand-native

You're now using multiple actual Algorand primitives naturally:

**USDC ASA.** Algorand assets are protocol-native ASAs rather than token smart contracts; receiving an ASA requires account opt-in. MainNet USDC for this challenge is ASA `31566704`. ([Algorand Developer Portal][6])

**x402/AVM settlement.** The paid HTTP API settles through Algorand.

**ARC-4 / ARC-56.** Your Oracle app exposes a standardized ABI and gets a generated application specification for typed clients. ([Algorand Developer Portal][7])

**ARC-21.** You're literally implementing Algorand's oracle convention. ([Algorand Developer Portal][1])

**Boxes.** Historical compact oracle snapshots. ([Algorand Developer Portal][8])

**ARC-28 logs.** Machine-readable on-chain events.

**AlgoKit Subscriber.** Durable event ingestion from your own application. ([Algorand Developer Portal][9])

**Atomic transaction groups.** Algorand supports protocol-native all-or-nothing groups of up to 16 transactions; this is useful later for economic workflows that must combine transfers and app calls atomically. ([Algorand Developer Portal][10])

**State Proofs.** Later, your proof endpoint can expose Algorand's network-generated State Proof context for extremely strong external verification of the chain history containing your Oracle commitments. ([Algorand Developer Portal][11])

That is plenty. Don't invent a token.

